WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.777
This PostgreSQL preview shows how storage and validation can work together.

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:06.632
The table requires patient ID and encounter date.

00:00:06.632 --> 00:00:09.352
Triage level must belong to an approved list.

00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:11.832
Temperature uses a teaching range.

00:00:11.832 --> 00:00:13.952
Emergency visits and total charge cannot be negative.

00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:16.837
Run the valid example first.

00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:21.062
Then change emergency visits to negative one or use an unapproved triage label.

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:23.297
PostgreSQL should reject the invalid row.

00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:29.852
That is an example of moving a documented validity rule into the database itself.

00:00:29.852 --> 00:00:31.657
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:37.817
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:39.407
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:46.212
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data,

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:51.432
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:52.792
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:58.000
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
